should be rejected with clear error messages | `"full,invalid"` is accepted 
because it contains `"full"`, even though `"invalid"` is not a valid range |\n| 
Validation should be consistent: `"invalid"` alone is rejected, but 
`"full,invalid"` is accepted | Inconsistent validation creates confusion and 
potential bugs |\n| Invalid content should not be silently ignored | The 
`continue` statements skip invalid parts without warning, allowing garbage data 
|\n\n## Root Cause\nThe validation logic in `_validate_lines_read` has the 
condition: `if not has_full and not has_valid_range and normalized:`. This means
if `has_full` is `True` (string contains `"full"`), the validation passes 
regardless of whether there are other invalid parts. The comment says "If we 
have \'full\', it\'s valid regardless of other valid ranges", but `"invalid"` is
not a "range" at all - it\'s invalid content that should be rejected.\n\n## Fix 
Plan\nChange the validation logic to reject strings containing invalid non-range
content even when `"full"` is present. Add a `has_invalid_part` flag that tracks
whether any invalid parts were encountered during parsing. If `has_invalid_part`
is `True`, raise `ValueError` regardless of whether `"full"` is present.\n\n## 
Status: FIXED\n\n## Files Changed\n1. `src/henchman/agents/working_memory.py` - 
Updated `_validate_lines_read` method to track invalid parts and reject strings 
containing invalid content\n2. 
`tests/agents/test_working_memory_full_contains_bug.py` - Updated test 
expectations to match new behavior (reject `"full,additional info"`)\n3. 
`tests/agents/test_working_memory_parse_bug.py` - Updated test expectations to 
match new behavior (reject `"10-50,0-5,60-100"`)\n4. 
`tests/agents/test_working_memory_validation_bug_fix.py` - Updated test 
expectations to match new behavior (reject `"full,invalid-format"`)\n5. 
`tests/agents/test_working_memory_validation_bug.py` - New test created to 
verify the fix (expects rejection of `"full,invalid"`)\n\n## Fix Details\nThe 
fix adds a `has_invalid_part` flag that is set to `True` when any invalid part 
is encountered (non-decimal characters, invalid line numbers, etc.). If 
`has_invalid_part` is `True`, the validation raises `ValueError` with message 
"Invalid lines_read \'{lines_read}\': contains invalid content". This ensures 
that strings containing any invalid content are rejected, even when `"full"` is 
present.'})
